# Public changelog

`src/server/site/changelog.js` is the single source of truth for the changelog at
`/changelog`. It renders on both the Vercel build and the Express route.

## Keep it short

The changelog is for **customers**, not a commit log. Prefer fewer entries. If the
page would take more than a short scroll, you added too much — merge or cut.

## When to update it

Add an entry only for:

- New capabilities a server owner would use (panel, Discord bot, billing plans)
- Pricing / plan limit changes that match the marketing site
- User-facing **bug fixes** that broke something they rely on (bans, RCON, login, checkout)

**Skip** (do not changelog):

- Copy tweaks, label renames, legal/docs wording polish
- Design tokens, spacing, fonts, layout restyles
- Ops / internal tooling (maintenance mode, alerts, Sentry, runbooks)
- SEO, a11y, performance, refactors, dependency bumps, tests, CI

When in doubt, skip. Batch small related fixes into one entry later if they matter.

## How to update it

Add to the **top** of `entries`. Reuse today's entry if one already exists for the
same date and it hasn't shipped yet; otherwise start a new one. Prefer **one entry
per day** with a handful of bullets over many titled posts.

```js
{
  date: "2026-08-04", // ISO, renders as "August 4, 2026"
  title: "Scheduled broadcasts", // short, sentence case, no version numbers
  changes: [
    { type: "added", text: "Queue broadcasts to send on a repeating timer." },
    { type: "fixed", text: "Ban list paging skipped the last page." },
  ],
}
```

- `type` is only `added`, `changed`, or `fixed`.
- Write `text` for a server owner, not a developer: describe the capability, not the
  implementation. Say "Staff can now be limited to kick-only" rather than "refactored
  the permission resolver".
- One sentence per change, ending in a period. Inline HTML like `<code>` is allowed.

Do this in the same change as the feature, before committing — only when the bar above
is met.
